  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA ridge building into the Northern Plains with trailing thermal\nridge will result in well above average high temperatures \ntoday, becoming unseasonably warm and near record territory \nThursday through Friday. These highs Thursday and Friday will \nrange mostly from the 70s to low 80s with lows mostly in the 50s\nThursday night (mid 40s far northwest). Dry conditions are \nexpected to accompany this feature through Thursday night. After\nwhich, the current NBM keeps then keeps things mostly dry \nduring the day Friday before low rain chances return Friday \nnight possibly lingering into Saturday morning. That said, as \nupper low pressure over the northern Rockies lifts towards south\ncentral Canada Friday, associated shortwave energy and surface \nwarm frontal boundary could produce a few showers and possibly a\nfew thunderstorms Friday afternoon.\n\nBreezy southerly winds will likely develop Friday before a \nstrong cold frontal boundary brings windy conditions to much of\nthe state Friday night and possibly into Saturday. While the \nFROPA is currently favored to pass through at night, and \ntherefore not the most ideal timeframe for tapping into the \nstrongest winds aloft, current deterministic models contain \nmoderate pressure rises that could aid in winds reaching \nadvisory criteria anyways. Furthermore, should the front either \ncome through quicker or slower than currently forecast, which is\nalways a distinct possibility, then wind impacts could become \nhigher in the end.\n\nBehind the cold front, much cooler air is anticipated to filter\nin for this weekend into next week with highs Saturday mostly \nin the mid 40s to mid 50s, and highs mostly in the 40s on Sunday\nand Monday. A slight warming trend is then forecast into the \nmiddle of next week. Widespread lows in the 20s to low 30s are \nprogged Saturday night through Monday night. However, any \nnights where clouds clear and winds go relatively light, \ntemperatures will likely tank and become even cooler than the \nNBM currently forecasts.",
